## Title

**Secure, Remotely Accessible Multi-Compartment Delivery Storage System**

## Abstract

A secure delivery storage system includes a powered storage unit defining at least one ambient-temperature compartment and at least one cooled compartment. Each compartment includes a weight sensor configured to determine a weight of contents positioned within the respective compartment and a lockable access lid selectively movable between open and closed positions. A communications module enables the storage unit to receive remotely transmitted access codes and to communicate compartment status information to a remote computing system. A user interface, including a keypad, receives an entered access code. A controller validates the entered access code based on access-code data received through the communications module and, upon successful validation, selectively unlocks one or more of the access lids. The controller may further monitor compartment temperature, weight changes, lid position, and access events and communicate corresponding information to the remote computing system.

## Detailed Description

A secure delivery system comprises a powered storage unit configured to receive and retain delivered items, including items requiring different storage conditions. The storage unit includes a housing defining a plurality of separately accessible compartments. The plurality of compartments includes at least one ambient compartment configured to store items at or near an external ambient temperature and at least one cooled compartment having a refrigeration or thermoelectric cooling assembly configured to maintain an internal temperature below the external ambient temperature.

Each compartment is associated with a respective weighing device, such as a load cell or electronic scale, configured to generate weight data representative of the weight of one or more items contained within the compartment. Weight measurements may be used to detect placement, removal, or unauthorized movement of an item.

Each compartment is accessible through a respective lid, door, or other closure member. Each closure member is operatively associated with an electronically controllable locking mechanism. The locking mechanisms may be independently actuated to permit selective access to individual compartments.

The storage unit further includes a communications module configured to exchange data with a remote computing system over one or more wired or wireless communication networks. The communications module may receive one-time, time-limited, user-specific, delivery-specific, or compartment-specific access codes generated or authorized by the remote computing system. The communications module may also transmit operational data, including weight measurements, temperature measurements, lid status, lock status, access-event records, and power-status information.

A user interface is disposed on or otherwise associated with the storage unit. The user interface includes a keypad configured to receive an access code entered by a delivery person, recipient, service provider, or other authorized user. A controller compares the entered access code with access-code validation information received through the communications module. When the entered access code satisfies one or more validation criteria, the controller actuates the locking mechanism of an authorized compartment. When validation is unsuccessful, the controller maintains the compartment in a locked condition and may record or transmit information concerning the unsuccessful access attempt.

The controller may determine, based on changes in weight detected by a compartment scale, whether an item has been deposited into or removed from the compartment. Such a determination may be associated with an access event and transmitted to the remote computing system to provide confirmation of delivery or retrieval.

## Exemplary Claims

1. **A secure delivery storage system comprising:**
   - a powered storage unit having a housing defining an ambient-temperature compartment and a cooled compartment;
   - a cooling assembly thermally coupled to the cooled compartment and configured to maintain the cooled compartment at a temperature below an external ambient temperature;
   - a first weight sensor associated with the ambient-temperature compartment and configured to generate first weight data indicative of contents of the ambient-temperature compartment;
   - a second weight sensor associated with the cooled compartment and configured to generate second weight data indicative of contents of the cooled compartment;
   - a first lockable access closure associated with the ambient-temperature compartment;
   - a second lockable access closure associated with the cooled compartment;
   - a communications module configured to receive access-code information from a remote computing system;
   - a keypad configured to receive an entered access code; and
   - a controller operatively coupled to the first and second weight sensors, the first and second lockable access closures, the communications module, and the keypad, the controller being configured to:
     - validate the entered access code based at least in part on the access-code information received from the remote computing system; and
     - selectively unlock at least one of the first or second lockable access closures in response to successful validation of the entered access code.

2. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the access-code information identifies a particular one of the ambient-temperature compartment or the cooled compartment, and wherein the controller is configured to unlock only the access closure associated with the identified compartment.

3. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the access code comprises a one-time access code that is invalidated after use.

4. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the access code is valid only during a predetermined time interval.

5. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the controller is configured to detect placement or removal of an item based on a change in weight data generated by the weight sensor associated with an unlocked compartment.

6. **The system of claim 5**, wherein the communications module is configured to transmit a delivery or retrieval confirmation to the remote computing system in response to the detected change in weight.

7. **The system of claim 1**, further comprising at least one temperature sensor disposed within the cooled compartment, wherein the controller is configured to regulate the cooling assembly based on temperature data generated by the at least one temperature sensor.

8. **The system of claim 7**, wherein the communications module is configured to transmit an alert when the temperature of the cooled compartment is outside a predetermined temperature range.

9. **The system of claim 1**, further comprising a closure-position sensor associated with each lockable access closure, the controller being configured to determine whether each access closure is open or closed.

10. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the controller is configured to generate an access-event record including at least one of an entered access code, an access time, an identified compartment, a weight measurement, a temperature measurement, or a lock status.

11. **A method for controlling access to a secure delivery storage unit**, the method comprising:
   - remotely receiving access-code information at a communications module of the storage unit;
   - receiving, through a keypad of the storage unit, an access code entered by a user;
   - validating the entered access code using the remotely received access-code information;
   - identifying, based on the validated access code, an authorized compartment selected from an ambient-temperature compartment and a cooled compartment;
   - electronically unlocking an access closure of the authorized compartment;
   - measuring, using a scale associated with the authorized compartment, a change in weight within the authorized compartment; and
   - transmitting information indicative of the change in weight to a remote computing system.

12. **The method of claim 11**, further comprising relocking the access closure after detecting that the access closure has been closed.

13. **The method of claim 11**, further comprising invalidating the entered access code after the access closure has been unlocked or after a delivery event has been detected.

14. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium** storing instructions that, when executed by a controller of a delivery storage unit, cause the controller to:
   - receive remotely transmitted access-code information;
   - compare an access code entered through a keypad with the access-code information;
   - determine a compartment authorized by the access code;
   - actuate an electronic lock associated with the authorized compartment; and
   - determine, from weight data generated by a scale associated with the authorized compartment, whether an item has been placed into or removed from the authorized compartment.
